# The Exsecutor logo's 3D model, rendered by the language it compiles.
#
# examples/signaculum/ is now three files in two layers. forma.exsc is the
# PURE core: signaculum_pingue(exsg, fb, zb) takes the whole fixed-point
# description of logo/Meshy_AI_Crossed_Ascension_0910024256 -- 1493
# vertices, 2981 faces, baked per-face flat colours -- as ONE 44,801-byte
# buffer (tests/data/signaculum_mesh.bin, written by the producer
# prototypes/signaculum_mesh.py), rasterises it at 256x256 with a 1/z
# z-buffer, backface culling and incremental edge functions into
# CALLER-OWNED framebuffer and z-buffer borrows, and returns a status
# (0 rendered; 1 bad magic; 2 bad counts; 3 short stream; 4 trailing
# bytes) -- the engine entry point Kiln consumes with a ROM, no stream.
# signaculum.exsc is the stream skin: a pump that copies stdin into the
# buffer, hands over, and writes the binary P6 PPM out of the framebuffer.
# ascensus.exsc is the unchanged initium driver. Every float the program
# holds is delivered as an integer on the 2^-23 grid and decoded by
# division by 8388608.0 -- a power of two, so the f64 quotient is EXACT
# on both sides and the program and oracle hold identical f64 bits from
# the first operation on.
#
# The golden is expected.out, produced by prototypes/signaculum_oracle.py,
# the independent second implementation: it MIRRORS the program's f64
# operation order line for line, so under round-to-nearest-even (the
# 0x1F80 MXCSR image both backends start with) the two either agree byte
# for byte or one side's operation order changed. Both backends are held
# to it: the run phase below, then the differential phase over gcc/clang
# at -O0/-O2.
#
# (No stdout= on purpose: expected.out IS the reference; naming both is
# the run.sh failure for "which of the two governs?".)
TEST: expect-exit=0 sources=examples/signaculum/forma.exsc,examples/signaculum/signaculum.exsc,examples/signaculum/ascensus.exsc stdin=tests/data/signaculum_mesh.bin cross=yes
